WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.262
Here is the engine inside our browser video editor, and what makes it faster.

2
00:00:04.612 --> 00:00:09.416
FFmpeg is the standard open-source tool for decoding, filtering and encoding video.

3
00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:14.072
ffmpeg.wasm compiles it to WebAssembly, so it runs inside the browser tab.

4
00:00:14.122 --> 00:00:15.938
The video never has to be uploaded.

5
00:00:16.288 --> 00:00:20.441
But the upstream release is unmaintained. It was built in early 2025.

6
00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:26.096
Our fork rebuilds it with a current Emscripten and FFmpeg 8, with every source pinned by commit and checksum.

7
00:00:26.446 --> 00:00:28.814
JavaScript runs one thread per context.

8
00:00:28.864 --> 00:00:32.989
WebAssembly threads are Web Workers that share one memory: a SharedArrayBuffer.

9
00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:37.603
That lets decoding, filtering and encoding run on several CPU cores at once.

10
00:00:37.653 --> 00:00:43.317
It requires a cross-origin isolated page. Otherwise, the editor falls back to a single thread.

11
00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:48.037
The key bug we found: FFmpeg ran on the module's main thread, and blocked it.

12
00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:51.683
But in the browser, a new thread needs that main thread to create its Worker.

13
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:55.441
Once a job needed more than the 32 pre-spawned threads, it deadlocked.

14
00:00:55.491 --> 00:00:59.802
Four-core cloud test machines never reproduced it. A 14-core laptop did.

15
00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:04.724
The fix: run each job on its own thread, so the main thread stays free to start Workers.

16
00:01:05.074 --> 00:01:11.943
On an Apple M4 Pro, in Chromium, joining three clips with a crossfade used to hang. Now it takes about three seconds.

17
00:01:11.993 --> 00:01:20.825
Re-encoding ten seconds of 1080p video: about 14 seconds single-threaded, 5.3 multithreaded on the old build, and 4.1 on the fork.

18
00:01:21.175 --> 00:01:27.678
Then we switched to upstream x264, and fixed a bug that made WebAssembly trap beyond about six encoder threads.

19
00:01:27.728 --> 00:01:30.487
Before, the editor had to cap the encoder at four.

20
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:38.102
Now it scales: 9.8 seconds on one thread, 3.9 on four, 2.3 on eight, 1.6 on fourteen.

21
00:01:38.152 --> 00:01:40.876
Early, single-run numbers, on one machine.

22
00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:46.323
The old multithreaded core reserved a fixed one-gigabyte heap, which iOS Safari refuses.

23
00:01:46.373 --> 00:01:51.370
The fork starts at 256 megabytes and grows on demand, up to four gigabytes.

24
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.945
And 60 jobs in a row now pass, on Chromium, WebKit and Firefox.

25
00:01:56.295 --> 00:01:59.679
SIMD means one instruction processes several values at once.

26
00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:03.680
Today, the compiler auto-vectorizes loops into WebAssembly SIMD.

27
00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:09.137
But the hand-written assembly that makes native builds fast is x86 and ARM only, so it is off here.

28
00:02:09.187 --> 00:02:16.436
We turned on FFmpeg's own WebAssembly SIMD code. Today it covers only HEVC decoding, and it gained about three percent.

29
00:02:16.486 --> 00:02:19.474
So the speed here comes from threads, not from SIMD.

30
00:02:19.824 --> 00:02:22.302
So, for you: no upload round trip.

31
00:02:22.352 --> 00:02:23.972
All the cores your device has.

32
00:02:24.022 --> 00:02:25.040
Bigger clips fit.

33
00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.573
And jobs no longer hang.

34
00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:32.038
One caveat: these numbers come from one machine, and Safari on real iPhones still needs testing.
